  saying  it's going to prevent united states  companies exporting  that sort of surveillance technology to  xinjiang  okay adrian brown there live for us in  hong kong thank you very much  indeed well let's get more reaction from  china now einer tengan is a political  analyst and government adviser and joins  us now  from beijing uh i know we've been  hearing there that china is  um has already come out with an angry  response  uh just why is china so angry at the u.s  wanting to play its role as a moral  leader  um i think uh the hypocrisy of saying  that  the u.s is in a position to be a moral  leader at this point  given what's going on in the middle east  and uh other places uh  you know it really strikes a chord  within china but  most of all this is uh they seen as a  continuing series of attacks  um really aimed not so much at freeing  uh anybody or human rights but as  putting pressure  on china as its these negotiations these  trade negotiations going forward  now that having said that that i believe  is the trump uh  administration's point of view if you  start looking beneath that uh at the  democratic side and the fact that this  was bipartisan  it seems the only thing that uh people  in the u.s in  politics and washington can agree on is  that china is somehow an evil empire  i don't know if that's necessarily the  case china is not  really doing anything differently than  it has done  for many many years but because of its  sheer size and the  implications of it becoming a uh first  the second world power and potentially  the first world power you're starting to  see a lot of friction  but isn't it possible i know tangent  that this isn't about  um getting an upper hand with trades  that people  are genuinely horrified by the  allegations of what china is doing to  the ethnic uighur population  well i mean there's two sides of the  story i mean and then there's always  going to be a divide on this because on  one side  in the west we view the rights 